"Charging by Induction | VirtualDemos",
"Charging by Induction",
"Two metal spheres on insulating stands, A (radius {#} cm) and B, and a charged rod: glass rubbed with silk (positive) or rubber rubbed with fur (negative). Everything works by hand on the picture: drag the rod anywhere near the spheres, slide either sphere along the bench (pushed together, they touch), and touch a sphere with the ground wand to ground it. The color on each sphere and the marks around it change as you move things, and moving dots show electrons flowing through the contact, a wire or the ground lead (drawn slowed down). Each setup also has a sequence of steps (Next step, Start over) that moves the apparatus for you; changing anything yourself leaves the steps. Changing a control keeps the charges the spheres already have.",
"The color on each sphere and the marks around it show the surface charge density; the readouts give the net charge on each sphere and its potential, as an electrometer would. Record reading stores the setup, the rod’s charge and distance, B’s radius, the gap, the connections, both net charges and \\(R/(R+d)\\); Export to worksheet sends the readings here.",
"Charging by rubbing: choose a rod (glass, hard rubber, Teflon) and a cloth (fur, wool, silk), then rub once or ten times. Record reading there stores the rod, the cloth, the number of strokes, both charges, the electrons moved, the surface charge density on the rubbed end and the field just outside it; a series in which every reading is from Charging by rubbing goes to the Rubbing table.",
"Blind hides the net charges and potentials, leaving the marks. Keep it off for this lab.",
"Perfect conductors, the rod’s charge as a point charge at its tip, \\(k = 1/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0)\\). A grounded sphere is held at zero potential; a sphere on its own stand keeps its net charge; joined spheres share one potential. The charges are found exactly with Kelvin’s image charges at every position, including the pull of each sphere on the other’s charge. Touching spheres are solved {#} mm apart. The bench, stands, wand and leads are left out of the electrostatics. The readouts also give the strongest field at each sphere’s surface; above about \\(3 \\times 10^6\\ \\text{V/m}\\) air would spark.",
"Charging by rubbing: electrons move from the material nearer the fur end of the series to the one nearer the Teflon end, so rod and cloth carry equal and opposite charges. The rubbed patch can hold at most \\(\\lvert Q\\rvert = \\varepsilon_0E_b\\,(2\\pi rL)\\), where the field just outside, \\(\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0\\), reaches air’s breakdown field \\(E_b = 3\\times10^6\\) V/m (OpenStax Vol. {#}, Table {#}); for \\(r = 0.5\\) cm and \\(L = 5\\) cm that is {#} nC. Each stroke closes a share \\(0.12\\times\\lvert s\\rvert\\) of the gap between the rod’s charge and that limit, where \\(s\\) is how far apart the two materials sit in the series ({#} for the two ends): a stated model, not measured data. Real results depend on humidity and clean surfaces.",
"Charge a metal sphere without touching it, measure how the charge it gets depends on the rod’s charge and distance, and compare with the image charge result. Compare charging by induction with charging by contact.",
"In a conductor, free electrons move until the field inside is zero, so a conductor in equilibrium is at one potential. A charged rod near a neutral sphere pulls charge of the opposite sign to the near side and pushes the same sign to the far side: the sphere is polarized but still neutral.",
"If the sphere is connected to ground while the rod is near, charge flows to or from the Earth until the sphere is at zero potential. For a lone sphere of radius \\(R\\) with a point charge \\(q\\) at distance \\(D = R + d\\) from its center (\\(d\\) from its surface), the sphere at zero potential carries exactly",
"\\[Q = -q\\,\\frac{R}{D} = -q\\,\\frac{R}{R+d}.\\]",
"(This is Kelvin’s image charge: the field outside the grounded sphere is that of \\(q\\) and a charge \\(-qR/D\\) at distance \\(R^2/D\\) from the center.) If the ground is disconnected first and the rod is then removed, the sphere keeps this charge, which spreads evenly over it. Removing the rod first lets the charge flow back, and the sphere ends up neutral.",
"Two touching spheres are one conductor. Separated in the rod’s field, they keep equal and opposite charges. When a charged sphere touches a neutral one, charge flows until both are at the same potential. Joined by a long wire far apart, each sphere has \\(V = kQ/R\\), so the charges share in the ratio of the radii; touching, each sphere’s charge pushes on the other’s and the shares are different.",
